in preparation for most sermons and in  particular this one I read a lot of  other people's thoughts on this text I  read other sermons I skimmed many  commentaries most of them had really  cute titles like quite a catch fishing  for Christ or by far the most popular  gone fishing well those get a little  cheesy for my taste but the one that  caught my attention was get out of here  hmm I don't like to judge a book by its  cover or a commentary by its title so I  read on and discovered that will  Willimon Methodist bishop had written  about this very gospel text a few years  ago in the Christian century he  commented on how the crowd was pushing  in on Jesus finally starting to maybe  understand what he was about attracted  to his sermons and his words but there  was Simon Simon witness to this crazy  unbelievable miracle and all Simon could  say is get away from me Lord or Willem  ins translation get out of here well get  out of here seems maybe the most  appropriate response because what do we  normally say when we hear the crazy  unbelievable too good to be true  miracles in the gospel get out of here  or what do we say when we hear Jesus  calling us out to the deep water get out  of here a sarcastic yet quite honest  response that we might find ourselves  saying quite often to the call of God  when I was a freshman in college my  chaplain pulled me aside the beginning  of the second semester and said have you  considered seminary well it had been a  long semester of fishing for the right  major not catching anything cleaning my  Nets after final exams wondering if all  of the work was just in vain but I was  also very content in my little small  boat with my communications major and my  tightly held dreams to be on the radio  someday  but I think God was speaking through  chaplain Brian that day when he nudged  me to consider exploring ministry well  have you thought about seminary he said  again my quick and baffled answer was uh  no translation get out of here and then  I had to also ask what seminary he  explained more and I said no no thank  you I'm very content in my small boat  with my communications major in my  tightly held dreams to be on the radio  but after each semester when I was  cleaning my Nets wondering if my work  was in vain he would find yet another  creative way to drop this hint subtly or  not so subtly so we were cleaning up  from an evening service he would say  well we are offering a course this  winter on exploring careers in ministry  in case you're interested my silence  became my way of saying get out of here  but simultaneously there was a snagging  curiosity growing and I hardly noticed  that I was gradually and repeatedly  being invited to go out to the deep  water this invitation came not only from  chaplain Brian but another voice that I  didn't understand a voice that promised  risk and also hope a voice that assured  me I wasn't going to do it alone but  this pull out to deep water was a much  slower process than for Simon &amp; Company  it took years it's still going on I  don't think deep water is a place we can  ever get too comfortable there are too  many dangers out there too much unknown  but before we know it the tide has  pushed us back to the shore back to a  place of the safe secure shallow water  until God once again calls us out into  the deep and we have to decide yet again  how we will respond I'm not going to  bore you with the rest of the story of  how i got into ministry and i do want to  make it really clear that following the  call of Jesus does not necessarily mean  pursuing professional ministry in fact  another mentor in college turned to me  when he heard I was consider  this and said if you can do anything  else besides be a pastor you should do  it he was a little burned out himself  but he was also saying if God is  inviting you to the deep waters of some  other calling you better get in that  boat the boat made for you well when it  comes to the call of the disciples by  the lakeshore each of our four Gospels  tells a similar story but emphasizes  different details which I think are  really important in Matthew and Mark we  hear a very short story about the call  of Simon and Andrew brothers called to  be disciples Jesus gets right to the  point he walks up to them they are  fishing off the shore and he just says  follow me I'll make you fish for people  that's it they dropped their nets Jesus  continues down the shore to James and  John follow me I'll make you fish for  people they drop their nets that's it  the Gospel of John never as brief Jesus  stands on the shore and calls out to the  disciples out there in the fishing boat  hey have you caught any fish no we'll  just try throwing the nets in on the  other side of the boat and they were  full that's how it happens for John and  instead of Simon saying get out of here  or some version of that Simon actually  gets out of there himself he is so  stunned in the Gospel of John that he  jumps out of the boat but Luke chooses  to tell it another way Jesus gets into  the boat with Simon Jesus is back on the  beach in the other Gospels he's in the  boat in Luke and he tells Simon to row  out to the deep water the only time in  the four Gospels that it involves deep  water Simon explains they've been  fishing all night and haven't caught a  thing but agrees to give it a try and  they instantly catch a bunch of fish and  we know that Simon is so in all of the  presence of Jesus that he tells him get  out of here he feels unworthy to be in  the presence of God in the presence of  this miracle worker  Jesus but instead of Jesus just saying  follow me as we hear in Matthew and Mark  Jesus instead tell Simon do not be  afraid for now on you will be catching  people so the gift of having these four  slightly different versions in the  Gospels is that we have at least four  different lenses through which we can  hear and see this story in this year of  reading Luke's Gospel together in this  day of hearing Luke's miraculous fishing  story Luke's lens gives us some unique  ways to consider discipleship now  discipleship is one of those words we  throw around a lot here at this church  we sing about being called disciples of  Jesus we pray for strength to do it we  hear texts like today's about the  disciples of Jesus and yet discipleship  might not be one of those words you say  ever outside of these walls the twelve  disciples can be put on such a pedestal  in our minds that we forget just how  imperfect just how much like us they are  thus the task of discipleship can seem  so inflated and become one of those I'll  never get it right and may as well not  try kinds of things and yet we are  somehow drawn back here to this place to  this text to ask what it means for us  Luke story of the miraculous catch of  fish and the invitation to Simon to take  up a different kind of fishing might be  able to help us decode the loaded word  of discipleship now I hate tidy  three-point sermons but somehow I have  distilled this next section down into  three tidy points okay discipleship in  Luke number one Jesus asks his disciples  to go into the deep water but goes with  them that is key if discipleship means  getting into your metaphorical boat and  going into your metaphorical deep waters  then it is crucial to remember that for  Luke the deep water happens only when  Jesus gets in the boat with the  ciples where do you sense the presence  of God is it sometimes in those moments  when you experience risk or stretching  out of your comfort zone Jesus rarely  calls us to the convenient comfortable  complacent shallow waters but Jesus  doesn't call you to places where he  hasn't gone or isn't willing to go your  deep water is going to be different than  anyone elses deep water being here this  morning might feel like you're being  thrown in the deep end completely  unfamiliar this might feel terribly  risky and for other people this might  feel right at home regardless of what  the deep water is for you here's the  truth for Luke if Jesus calls you to the  deep water he's going with you second  Jesus asks his disciples to be honest  and then calms fears so discipleship is  being honest with Jesus simon doesn't  pretend to be all holy when he witnessed  the miracle of the fish instead he just  blurted out his honest fear-filled truth  acknowledging that he didn't feel worthy  to be in the presence of jesus also  Jesus senses Simon's fear in this  response simon says get out of here and  Jesus says don't be afraid Jesus can  take your honesty even if it seems rude  so if you sense that God might be  nudging you in some direction that you  just can't imagine going you may as well  be honest wrestle it out with God a bit  get out of here for Simon was his honest  and actually a pretty humble response to  nets miraculously so full of fish when  Jesus gets in the boat you won't be able  to hide very much but your honesty will  be matched with Christ's fear calming  comfort and presence and third  discipleship might invite you to leave  those Nets behind even as they are  bursting with fish  yeah this the disciples just leave their  nets and follow him and this might sound  even scarier than the deep water it's  not logical for the disciples to leave  they did not sit down and calculate how  much that was going to cost them in  losses logic was left way back there in  the shallow water a long time ago for  them though it wasn't logical to follow  Jesus out into the deep water after a  night of pointless fishing was it  logical for them to leave their nets  bursting with finance and security was  not logical but Jesus isn't logical  discipleship might not be logical it's  not a matter of just rearranging the  pieces of our lives to make room for God  somehow discipleship is being willing to  walk away from all of those pieces to  follow Christ you might not be called to  leave family or business or goals behind  but discipleship calls us to be willing  to do that or at least keep all of those  pieces in perspective discipleship is a  willingness to follow Jesus into the  unknown and I know that's terribly vague  and abstract especially for those of you  grasping for some clear picture of what  your future is going to hold we list  pros and cons we get everyone's expert  opinion about what we should do we try  to please family members or advisors but  all of this may not in the end help you  feel certain poet Wendell Berry writes  it may be that when we no longer know  what to do we have come to our real work  and that when we no longer know which  way to go we have come to our real  journey so all I'm saying is that there  may be a time when you feel drawn to do  something for reasons you cannot explain  in ways that don't make financial sense  or please other people and that  might be exactly what you are supposed  to do amen
 

